.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt NOQTIS-SEAL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm noqtis-seal
.Nd seal a folder of files into a signed capsule
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm noqtis seal
.Fl k Ar key
.Op Fl p Ar key.pub
.Op Fl m Ar json-file
.Op Fl -format Cm 1 | 2
.Op Fl -input Oo Ar name Ns = Oc Ns Ar dir ...
.Op Fl -tsa Ar url ...
.Op Fl -anchor Ar der-file ...
.Op Fl r | Fl -recursive
.Ar dir
.Nm noqtis seal
.Fl -token
.Op Fl -module Ar path
.Op Fl -slot Ar n
.Fl -label Ar name | Fl -id Ar hex
.Op Fl -pin-fd Ar n | Fl -pin-env Ar name
.Op Fl m Ar json-file
.Op Fl -format Cm 1 | 2
.Op Fl -input Oo Ar name Ns = Oc Ns Ar dir ...
.Op Fl -tsa Ar url ...
.Op Fl -anchor Ar der-file ...
.Op Fl r | Fl -recursive
.Ar dir
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm noqtis seal
turns the folder
.Ar dir
into a capsule
.Pq Xr noqtis-capsule 5 .
It lists every regular file under
.Ar dir ,
computes each file's size, SHA-256 and SHA3-256, writes the manifest
.Pa noqtis.json
(canonical JSON, RFC 8785), signs its exact bytes with the secret key in
.Ar key
under the context string
.Ql noqtis-capsule-1
(format 2: its head, under
.Ql noqtis-capsule-2 ) ,
and writes the seal
.Pa noqtis.sig .
An existing
.Pa noqtis.json
and
.Pa noqtis.sig
at the top of
.Ar dir
are left out of the listing and replaced.
Both are written through a temporary file beside them, renamed into
place:
.Pa .noqtis.json.\& Ns Ar pid Ns Pa \&. Ns Ar k Ns Pa .tmp
(and the same of
.Pa noqtis.sig
and
.Pa noqtis.ers ;
.Ar pid
decimal digits,
.Ar k
0 to 15).
A file at the top of
.Ar dir
named exactly so, left by a seal that was stopped, is never listed, nor
is a file anywhere in
.Ar dir
named
.Pa .noqtis.<16 hex digits>.tmp ,
the name the tool's other outputs and the data record are written
through;
.Xr noqtis-check 1
still fails either as a file the manifest does not list, so remove it.
A seal stopped by a signal
.Xr noqtis 1
lists as handled under SIGNALS removes its own temporary files first:
the ones it made, never a file that had the name before.
.Pp
The new manifest and the new seal are both written whole under their
temporary names before either is renamed; then
.Pa noqtis.json
is renamed into place, and
.Pa noqtis.sig
last.
A re-seal that fails or is stopped while it writes leaves the old
capsule as it was.
From before the first file is written until the seal is whole (and
stamped, with
.Fl -tsa ) ,
every signal
.Xr noqtis 1
lists as handled under SIGNALS
.Po
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGQUIT ,
.Dv SIGPIPE ,
.Dv SIGTERM ,
.Dv SIGHUP ,
.Dv SIGXFSZ ,
.Dv SIGXCPU ,
.Dv SIGALRM ,
.Dv SIGUSR1 ,
.Dv SIGUSR2
and the others there
.Pc
puts back the
.Pa noqtis.json
and
.Pa noqtis.sig
the folder had, or remove the new ones where it had none, before the
command ends by the signal.
The two are put back together: each that changed is first written
whole under a new name of the shape
.Pa .noqtis.<16 hex digits>.tmp
(made with
.Dv O_EXCL ,
so no other file is ever written), and only then are both renamed into
place.
They are put back in the folder that was read, through a descriptor of
it opened before anything was written, so a folder renamed or replaced
by a link meanwhile is never written through.
A file put back keeps its permission bits, the set-user-ID, set-group-ID
and sticky bits too (set after its group, whose change would clear
them), and, where its owner may give it, its group; its hard links, ACLs and
extended attributes are not
kept, nor its owner when another user made it.
When they cannot be put back (a write over
.Ic ulimit -f ,
a folder that may not be written), neither is renamed, so the folder
keeps what the seal had written, and standard error says
.Ql noqtis: Ar dir : stopped, and its files could not be put back as they were: check it .
Only a signal that is not handled
.Pq Dv SIGKILL , No a fault such as Dv SIGSEGV ,
or a power cut, in the moment between the two renames, a failure of the
second, or such a failed put-back then, leaves the new
.Pa noqtis.json
beside the old
.Pa noqtis.sig :
.Xr noqtis-check 1
then fails
.Ql manifest.digest
and says to seal again, which is the remedy.
.Pp
A file that grows or shrinks while it is read (its size differs from the
one it had when the folder was listed) is never sealed: the seal fails
with exit status 2 and nothing new is written.
On success it prints
.Dl sealed Ar dir No with Ar alg
.Pp
With
.Fl -tsa ,
it then asks a time-stamping authority (TSA) for an RFC 3161 timestamp
over the seal's signature and stores the token in
.Pa noqtis.sig
.Pq Xr nqtsput 3 .
The request
.Pq Xr nqtsreq 3
holds only the SHA-256 of the signature, a random nonce and a request
for the TSA's certificate; it is sent by an HTTP/1.1 POST
.Pq Ql application/timestamp-query ,
and the reply must be a granted token over that signature with that
nonce, signed by a TSA certificate valid at its genTime.
It prints
.Dl timestamp from Ar url : No genTime Ar time
and the summary line ends in
.Ql and a timestamp .
A seal holds one timestamp: give
.Fl -tsa
again for fallbacks, tried in order until one gives a token that checks.
When none does, the new
.Pa noqtis.json
and
.Pa noqtis.sig
are removed, so the folder is never left sealed without the timestamp
that was asked for, and the exit status is 2; a seal the folder had
before is put back as it was.
A handled signal while it waits (Ctrl-C,
.Dv SIGTERM ,
.Dv SIGHUP ,
a standard error that is a closed pipe when the failure is reported,
and the others of SIGNALS in
.Xr noqtis 1 )
does the same; only a signal that is not handled
.Pq Dv SIGKILL , No a fault
or a power cut can leave the folder sealed without one.
A TSA has 120 seconds in all for one request, the name lookup included
(30 seconds at most for any one step).
.Pp
A folder with an evidence record
.Pq Pa noqtis.ers , Xr noqtis-ers 1
over its present seal is refused (exit 2): a new seal would replace what
the record proves; move
.Pa noqtis.ers ,
.Pa noqtis.sig
and
.Pa noqtis.json
out together first.
.Pp
The folder must not hold a noqtis secret key (a file that starts with
.Ql noqtis-key-1 <alg> sec ) ,
which would ship with the capsule: the seal is refused, with exit status 2.
.Pp
The folder must hold only regular files and folders with portable names.
Nothing is written, and the exit status is 2, when it holds a symbolic
link, a Windows reparse point, a device, pipe or socket, a name that is
empty, holds a control character,
.Ql / ,
.Ql \e
or
.Ql \&: ,
is not valid UTF-8, or two paths that differ only in ASCII case; or when it
exceeds a limit: more than 2,000,000 files, a path over 4096 bytes, folders
more than 64 levels deep, a manifest over 512 MiB, or a file over
2^53\-1 bytes.
.Pp
The creation time in the manifest is
.Ev SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
when it is set (a decimal number of seconds), otherwise the clock.
.Pp
The secret key and the public key must be one pair: the new seal is
verified before it is written, and a key pair that does not match is
refused.
.Pp
With
.Fl -token ,
the signature is made on a PKCS #11 token
.Pq Xr noqtis-token 1 , Xr nqp11seal 3 :
the secret key never leaves it, and the public key stored in the seal is
the one the token gives for that key.
.Fl k
and
.Fl p
are then refused, and so is a KEM key; every other option works as
without it.
.Pp
The secret key must not be under a passphrase: this command takes no
.Fl -pass-fd
or
.Fl -pass-env ,
so remove the passphrase first
.Pq Xr noqtis-passwd 1 Fl -no-pass
and put it back after.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl k Ar key , Fl -key Ns = Ns Ar key
A secret signature key file: ML-DSA, SLH-DSA or a hybrid of ML-DSA with
Ed25519; Ed25519 alone is refused.
.It Fl p Ar key.pub , Fl -pub Ns = Ns Ar key.pub
The matching public key, stored in the seal.
Default:
.Ar key Ns Pa .pub .
.It Fl m Ar json-file , Fl -meta Ns = Ns Ar json-file
A JSON object (at most 1 MiB) stored, in canonical form, as the
manifest's
.Ql meta
field and covered by the signature.
noqtis does not interpret it.
.It Fl -format Cm 1 | 2
The capsule format
.Pq Xr noqtis-capsule 5 .
Format 1 (the default) signs the whole manifest.
Format 2 adds a Merkle tree over the file entries and signs the
manifest's head, which holds the tree's root but not the list of files,
so that
.Xr noqtis-prove 1
can show some files to a reader without the names or digests of the
others.
.It Fl -input Oo Ar name Ns = Oc Ns Ar dir
A sealed capsule this one was made from, up to 32 times; it implies
.Fl -format Cm 2 .
The input must check
.Pq Xr noqtis-check 1 ,
and its id (the SHA-256 of the bytes its seal signs) is recorded under
.Ar name ,
by default the folder's last component (1 to 255 bytes, no control
characters).
With
.Fl -format Cm 1
it is a usage error.
.Xr noqtis-check 1
.Fl -lineage
follows these records back.
.It Fl -tsa Ar url
An RFC 3161 time-stamping authority, as
.Ql http:// Ns Ar host Ns Oo : Ns Ar port Oc Ns Op / Ns Ar path ,
up to 32 times.
Plain HTTP is enough: the token is signed by the TSA, so TLS adds no
integrity.
.Ql https://
works when noqtis was built with its own TLS 1.3 client (the CMake
option
.Dv NQTLS ) ,
the server's certificate checked against the PEM bundle
.Ev SSL_CERT_FILE
names, else the system's, and its Certificate Transparency SCTs against
the log list
.Ev NOQTIS_CT_LOGS
names, when set (see
.Xr noqtis-check 1 ) ;
otherwise it is refused with that said.
Most public TSAs answer on
.Ql http:// .
There is no proxy support.
Each connection, read and write waits at most 30 seconds.
.It Fl -anchor Ar der-file
The TSA's root or issuing certificate (DER), up to 32: the token must
then also have a certificate path to one of them, checked as
.Xr noqtis-check 1
.Fl -anchor
checks it.
Without it the reply is checked, but not whether the TSA is one to
trust, and a note on standard error says so.
Without
.Fl -tsa
it is a usage error.
.It Fl r , Fl -recursive
Seal, with the same options, every folder under
.Ar dir
(and
.Ar dir
itself) that holds
.Pa noqtis.json
(a capsule sealed before) or
.Pa noqtis.data.json
(a data record), deepest first, so that a capsule inside another is
sealed before the one that holds it.
The search stops 64 levels down and never follows a link: each folder is
opened from the one above it without following links, when it is listed
and again when it is sealed, so a link to a folder inside
.Ar dir
(on Windows, a reparse point),
.Ar dir
itself as a link, or a folder replaced by a link in between is refused,
never sealed through: it is named on standard error, counted as failed,
and nothing behind it is read.
.Ar dir
comes last.
Standard error ends with how many were sealed and failed; the exit
status is the worst of them, and 2 when a link was refused.
A capsule that fails is left as it was, and the others are sealed.
Until the last capsule is done, the earlier seal of every capsule
resealed so far is kept, with its folder open: a handled signal at any
moment of the run, between two capsules too (a message or a
.Ql sealed
line written to a pipe whose reader is gone, as in
.Ql noqtis seal -r tree | head ,
is
.Dv SIGPIPE ) ,
puts each of them back as described above, so a tree that passed
.Xr noqtis-check 1
.Fl r
before still does.
The command raises its soft limit of open files to the hard limit for
this, and needs one descriptor per capsule to seal plus 16 for the rest
of the run (the walk of the tree first needs two per folder level): a
tree with more capsules than that is refused before anything is
written, with a message naming how many capsules the limit allows and
the
.Ic ulimit -n
to give.
When a capsule still finds no descriptor left
.Pq Er EMFILE , Er ENFILE ,
the run stops there, says so, and puts back every capsule it had
resealed, so the whole tree is as it was.
.It Fl -token
Seal with a signature key on a PKCS #11 token instead of
.Fl k :
an ML-DSA or SLH-DSA key.
An Ed25519 key alone is refused, as it is with
.Fl k .
.It Fl -module Ar path
With
.Fl -token :
the token's PKCS #11 module; default the
.Ev NOQTIS_PKCS11
variable.
.It Fl -slot Ar n
With
.Fl -token :
the slot of the token, when more than one is present.
.It Fl -label Ar name , Fl -id Ar hex
With
.Fl -token :
the key, by its label or by its ID in hexadecimal; exactly one key must
answer.
.It Fl -pin-fd Ar n , Fl -pin-env Ar name
With
.Fl -token :
the token's PIN, read from descriptor
.Ar n
or from the environment variable
.Ar name ,
never from the command line.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev NOQTIS_PKCS11
With
.Fl -token :
the path of the PKCS #11 module, when
.Fl -module
is not given.
.It Ev SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
Seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z to record as the creation time,
for reproducible capsules.
A value that is not a plain decimal number is refused.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Ar dir Ns Pa /noqtis.json
the manifest
.It Ar dir Ns Pa /noqtis.sig
the seal
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when the capsule was written (with its timestamp, when
.Fl -tsa
was given); 2 on a usage error, a link to a folder met by
.Fl r ,
keys that are not a signature key pair, with
.Fl -token
a key that is not one signature key on the token, a wrong PIN or a
token that refuses (its CKR number is printed),
an unsealable folder, a limit, a file that cannot be read or written, or
no TSA that gave a timestamp.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noqtis keygen -a ML-DSA-65 -o me
ML-DSA-65 key pair: me (secret), me.pub (public)
$ echo '{"project":"demo"}' > meta.json
$ noqtis seal -k me -m meta.json results
sealed results with ML-DSA-65
$ ls results
data  noqtis.json  noqtis.sig  report.txt
.Ed
.Pp
A reproducible capsule:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=1767225600 noqtis seal -k me results
sealed results with ML-DSA-65
.Ed
.Pp
With a timestamp from one of two authorities, checked at seal time:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noqtis seal -k me --tsa http://timestamp.digicert.com \e
    --tsa http://timestamp.sectigo.com \e
    --anchor digicert.der --anchor sectigo.der results
timestamp from http://timestamp.digicert.com: genTime 2026-10-29T09:34:03Z, verified under a TSA anchor
sealed results with ML-DSA-65 and a timestamp
.Ed
.Pp
With a key that stays on a token:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ export NOQTIS_PKCS11=/usr/lib/softhsm/libsofthsm2.so
$ noqtis token keygen -a ML-DSA-65 -n release -o release.pub \e
    --pin-env PIN
$ noqtis seal --token --label release --pin-env PIN results
sealed results with ML-DSA-65
$ noqtis check -p release.pub results
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-check 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-keygen 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-prove 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-token 1 ,
.Xr nqp11seal 3 ,
.Xr nqseal 3 ,
.Xr nqtsput 3 ,
.Xr noqtis-capsule 5
